# 85. Artemis Autonomous Operations Maturity Roadmap

Autonomous Operations must mature separately from automated-trading maturity in
Section 53.

Progress in operational autonomy never implicitly advances Live trading.

## Operations Stage 0 — Read-Only Operator

- Artemis can inspect approved system state
- Artemis can research
- Artemis can explain required changes
- No mutations

## Operations Stage 1 — Proposal Operator

- Artemis creates canonical Action Intents
- Artemis produces diffs and rollback plans
- Human executes changes manually or through normal UI

## Operations Stage 2 — Human-Approved Execution

- Artemis can execute approved tools
- Every material write requires exact approval
- Audit and rollback are verified

## Operations Stage 3 — Autonomous Reversible Operations

- Proven Class O1 actions may execute automatically
- User locks, limits, rollback and circuit breakers are operational
- No broad cross-domain autonomy

## Operations Stage 4 — Bounded Domain Operator

- Selected domains may use verified Class O2 autonomy
- Examples may include approved Agent configuration or approved public source
  lifecycle
- Canary, evaluation and domain-specific limits are operational

## Operations Stage 5 — Cross-Domain Coordinated Operator

- Artemis may coordinate changes across multiple approved domains
- Dependency-aware planning is verified
- Cross-domain blast-radius controls are verified
- Privileged and critical actions remain approval-gated

## Operations Stage 6 — Human-Operator Substitution for Approved Domains

Artemis may perform most routine operator work for explicitly approved domains
without routine human intervention.

Requirements include:

- Proven reliability
- Low unauthorized-action rate
- Verified rollback
- Complete audit
- Stable policy
- Prompt-injection resistance
- Cost controls
- Human locks
- Operations Kill Switch
- Clear exception and escalation handling

This stage does not grant constitutional, security-governance or Live-trading
authority.

## Operations Stage 7 — Governed Operational Self-Sufficiency

Artemis can identify, research, implement, verify and learn from routine approved
operational improvements across mature domains while humans primarily govern:

- Policy
- Exceptions
- High-risk approvals
- Security boundaries
- Major architecture changes
- Live financial authorization

Operational self-sufficiency must remain measurable and revocable.

## 85.1 No Stage Skipping

Each stage requires evidence appropriate to the domain.

A successful demonstration in one domain does not automatically promote another
domain.

## 85.2 Domain-Specific Maturity

TitanGold may simultaneously have, for example:

- Agent Settings at Operations Stage 4
- Data Hub source management at Stage 2
- Credential management at Stage 1
- Security configuration at Stage 0

Do not use one global maturity label to hide weaker domains.

